  "query_preview": "..\n\n -Meteorological Overview:\n\nA strong upper-level ridge builds overhead through Friday, \ndriving temperatures well above seasonal averages with a few \nclimate locations approaching daily records. Over the next couple \nof days, a weak to moderate pressure gradient will sustain breezy \nconditions across the Rocky Mountain Front and Central Plains.\nHowever, as a powerful Pacific trough approaches the West Coast \non Thursday, downstream amplification will pump the ridge axis and\nassociated surface high pressure well north into Canada, creating\na pattern resembling an omega block. As the surface high shifts \nnorthward, the cross-barrier gradient along the RMF relaxes, \ndecreasing winds. Simultaneously, the shifting gradient reorients\nto favor breezy conditions funneling through the valleys of SW \nMontana Friday and Saturday. \n\nThe omega block pattern will be short-lived as a sequence of \nenergetic low-pressure systems begins an assault on the West Coast\nand especially the Pacific Northwest. This initiates the \nbreakdown of our ridging through this weekend. By Saturday, the \nridge axis shifts east into the Central Plains as Montana \ntransitions to weak southwesterly flow aloft ahead of a weaker \nshortwave trough. Moisture with the weekend wave will be sparse, \nresulting in isolated chances of light precipitation largely \nfocused across SW Montana and the Rockies. Temperatures will \nremain marginal, keeping snow-levels high above 7kft. The primary \nsynoptic role of this initial system is knocking down regional \nheights and helping usher in some more seasonal conditions ahead \nof potential systems next week. \n\nForecast confidence struggles heading into next week, with \nguidance pointing toward an increasingly uncertain yet active \npattern. Rather than a singular high-impact storm, recent trends \nfavor more of a split-flow setup early in the week, shunting the \ndeepest moisture and strongest dynamic forcing to our south while \nushering a return to near- seasonal temperatures across our \nregion. Behind this initial transition, the broader pattern \nremains quite chaotic. While multiple weak Pacific waves are \nlikely to traverse the region through the week, bringing periodic \nlow-end chances for precipitation and mountain snow, ensemble \nspread remains very high, and no large or high-impact systems are \nfavored at this time. -JR\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nHigh confidence surrounds the unseasonable warmth through Friday,\nas well as the transition toward ridge breakdown and cooler \ntemperatures this weekend. Confidence is medium to high (50-60%) \nthat Saturday into Sunday will feature low-impact light \nprecipitation largely across SW Montana and the Rockies. Snow \nlevels will remain well above pass levels, and the scattered \nnature of the precipitation indicates most lower-elevation \nlocations will remain dry. \n\nConfidence has lowered regarding any significant or high-impact \nstorm system early next week. While there is moderate confidence \nin a return toward seasonable temperatures, model spread is \nsubstantial regarding how individual waves track and interact:\n\nScenario 1 (Active, Chaotic Pattern): A split-flow regime keeps \nthe primary moisture tap and strongest dynamics displaced to the \nsouth, while a series of weaker northern-branch shortwaves cross \nthe Northern Rockies. This brings periodic light precipitation and\nminor pass-level snow chances, but overall impacts remain minimal.\n\nScenario 2 (More Amplified or Consolidated Wave): A shortwave \ndigs more aggressively or consolidates into a broader trough over \nthe Plains. This would allow a stronger push of Canadian cold air \nand wrap-around moisture into Central Montana, potentially \nintroducing a brief window for more organized precipitation, \nthough confidence in this evolution remains low. -JR",
